Created a new web scraper to automate user exports of each Infobip account. The scaper stores each account export as a separate file on the local device. Using bash to bulk edit and combine all 104 CSV files.

----

h4. Adding the {{Account Name}} to each of the CSV files as a new column 

{{for i in *.csv; do awk 'FNR==1 {print "Account Name" "," $0} FNR>1 {split(FILENAME,a,"."); print a[1] "," $0 }' $i > ${i}.tmp ; mv ${i}.tmp ${i} ; done}}

h4. Combining all of the CSV files into a single file

{{awk 'NR==1 || FNR>1' *.csv > InfobipExportCombined.csv}}

{quote}I can check if the field exists before setting it as {{timestamp}}, but we will have this difference between the {{timestamp}} across documents. {quote}

I think it’s the best approach, if we can read datetime from {{<JSON object>.date}} → {{created}}, then we put the value as {{@timestamp}}.
And if the value cannot be retrieved, we don’t do anything and {{@timestamp}} is the same as we have now.
I’d say we don’t need the {{created}} field separately, if it’s value is copied to {{@timestamp}}

there are 2 actions that can improve performance:

* remove the join in mentioned above query to user table
** we have to find orders by search_token and user_id
** add user_id column in order_calculation table
** backfill the column
* reduce the calls to {{fraud/rideHailing/getOrderStatisticsForDriver}}
{{this.fraudOrderDataClient.fraudRideHailingGetOrderStatisticsForDriver(ctx, {
    driver_id: request.driver_id,    from: this.getBefore(this.WEEK) as tUnixTimestamp
});}}
we can get them same data from {{BY_DAY}} stats, need to do it programmatically on the backend service

